In this recorded Codex session, the first
request audits published articles.

3
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:14,659
The follow-up asks which shared-image
fixes help the most articles.

4
00:00:14,659 --> 00:00:19,018
Codex computes a new repair plan within
the same permissions.

5
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:23,233
This saved rehearsal shows both turns.

6
00:00:23,233 --> 00:00:27,633
The follow-up rechecked forty-eight
articles and found three shared-image

7
00:00:27,633 --> 00:00:30,077
fixes, each helping two articles.

8
00:00:30,077 --> 00:00:33,499
Here is the exact PHP it submitted.

9
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:41,117
The host recorded ten upstream calls: four
list pages, four article batches

10
00:00:41,117 --> 00:00:43,057
and two media batches.

11
00:00:43,057 --> 00:00:47,420
Expand PHP execute for the actual request
and response.

12
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:53,515
A separate Symfony AI reference comparison
returns the same findings with ten

13
00:00:53,515 --> 00:00:56,992
calls each and eighty-five percent less
tool-result payload.

14
00:00:56,992 --> 00:00:59,600
That measures bytes, not model tokens.

15
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:03,818
These fixed probes ran real PHP in gVisor.

16
00:01:03,818 --> 00:01:05,577
An approved read succeeded.

17
00:01:05,577 --> 00:01:07,776
A delete call was denied.

18
00:01:07,776 --> 00:01:10,855
An infinite loop hit the two-second
watchdog.

19
00:01:10,855 --> 00:01:13,053
Each container was confirmed removed.

20
00:01:13,053 --> 00:01:16,572
The guest has no network or host
credentials.

21
00:01:16,572 --> 00:01:20,090
These checks are evidence, not a security
audit.

22
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:24,733
A reusable PHP package.

23
00:01:24,733 --> 00:01:27,167
Conversations stay in Codex.

24
